  "analysis": [
    {
      "layout": "P",
      "strengths": "English and Arabic have somewhat more room than in several other fitting layouts, and all five columns are visible at once.",
      "faults": "The Chinese column forces near-vertical reading, Japanese is heavily fragmented, and two headers wrap. Keys break repeatedly, and the long error row becomes exceptionally tall.",
      "spatial_logic": "Space is spread relatively evenly among the Latin and Arabic columns, leaving Chinese especially narrow. The resulting tall CJK cells determine the rhythm of the whole table."
    },
    {
      "layout": "Q",
      "strengths": "It is the most compact fitting layout shown and keeps the Chinese, Japanese and Arabic headers on one line. Its wider key column reduces some identifier fragmentation.",
      "faults": "English words and its header split, keys remain difficult to scan, and Arabic words also break. The error row is still very tall and difficult to read across.",
      "spatial_logic": "It favors the key and Japanese columns within the fixed viewport, accepting narrow English and Arabic columns. This reduces height compared with P and T but trades away word integrity."
    },
    {
      "layout": "R",
      "strengths": "Whole keys and words remain intact, the headers are clear, and most rows are short. The generous language columns make comparison comfortable in the full view.",
      "faults": "It requires the most horizontal scrolling. At first sight, the key consumes most of the viewport and the English column is cut off; a few Chinese and Arabic phrases wrap awkwardly despite the overall width.",
      "spatial_logic": "It gives each column enough room for relatively open text setting, prioritizing short rows and unbroken units over the amount of table visible on a phone."
    },
    {
      "layout": "S",
      "strengths": "The full key and English columns fit at first sight, making the initial view useful for lookup. It is the narrowest scrolling layout shown, and its language columns remain substantially more readable than those in the fitting layouts.",
      "faults": "One measured word break remains, and the narrow Chinese column produces less natural wrapping in longer entries. Several rows are taller than in R, while the other languages still require scrolling.",
      "spatial_logic": "It uses the exact key-plus-English viewport as a strong first screen, then compresses the remaining columns to reduce the scrolling distance."
    },
    {
      "layout": "T",
      "strengths": "Its wider key column gives identifiers more room, and all columns are initially visible. English is less constrained than in Q.",
      "faults": "Japanese and Arabic headers wrap, Arabic words split, and Japanese becomes a narrow vertical strip. The long error row is much taller than in Q, while keys still break inside identifiers.",
      "spatial_logic": "It concentrates scarce width on the key and English, taking it from Japanese and Arabic. The gains in the first two columns do not compensate for the severe fragmentation elsewhere."
    },
    {
      "layout": "U",
      "strengths": "Whole keys and measured words remain intact, and the full key and English columns fit at first sight. The wider Arabic column improves its longer entries compared with S.",
      "faults": "It requires substantial scrolling, and the Chinese shipping entry leaves a single character on its second line. English wraps several short phrases, making more rows tall than in R.",
      "spatial_logic": "It preserves S's useful first viewport while spending additional width on Chinese and Arabic to improve language readability."
    }
  ],
  "insights": "The long unbreakable keys make a fully readable five-column table impossible at phone width under the fixed styling. Among fitting layouts, Q is the strongest compromise, though its broken words and identifiers remain serious faults. Scrolling protects content integrity; S and U make especially good use of the first viewport by showing complete key and English columns, while R buys shorter rows at the cost of a partially visible English column. The best allocation depends on actual phrase breaks, not equal column widths or total height alone.",
